<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />

  <meta
    name="viewport"
    content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover"
  />

  <meta name="format-detection" content="telephone=no" />

  <title>HumanKey Protocol | Hash Humanity</title>

  <meta
    name="description"
    content="Learn how HumanKey uses liveness, biometric uniqueness checks, privacy-preserving proofs, and passkeys to support one human, one account on Hash Humanity."
  />

  <meta name="theme-color" content="#000000" />
  <meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large" />

  <link rel="icon" href="logo.png" />
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="logo.png" />

  <style>
    :root {
      --bg: #000;
      --text: #f5f5f7;
      --muted: #a1a1a6;
      --dim: #6e6e73;
      --line: rgba(255,255,255,.10);
      --line-strong: rgba(255,255,255,.18);
      --surface: rgba(255,255,255,.035);
      --max: 1180px;
      --reading: 780px;
    }

    * {
      box-sizing: border-box;
    }

    html {
      background: #000;
      scroll-behavior: smooth;
      overflow-x: hidden;
    }

    body {
      margin: 0;
      min-width: 320px;
      overflow-x: hidden;

      color: var(--text);

      background:
        radial-gradient(
          circle at 50% -4%,
          rgba(255,255,255,.065),
          transparent 25%
        ),
        #000;

      font-family:
        -apple-system,
        BlinkMacSystemFont,
        "SF Pro Display",
        "SF Pro Text",
        "Helvetica Neue",
        Arial,
        sans-serif;

      -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
      text-rendering: optimizeLegibility;
    }

    a {
      color: inherit;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    img {
      display: block;
      max-width: 100%;
    }

    button,
    a {
      -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
    }

    .wrap {
      width: min(calc(100% - 40px), var(--max));
      margin-inline: auto;
    }

    .reading {
      width: min(100%, var(--reading));
    }

    /* HEADER */

    .site-header {
      position: fixed;
      inset: 0 0 auto 0;
      z-index: 1000;

      padding-top: env(safe-area-inset-top);

      background: rgba(0,0,0,.76);

      backdrop-filter: blur(24px) saturate(140%);
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(24px) saturate(140%);

      border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.055);
    }

    .header-inner {
      height: 62px;

      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;

      gap: 12px;
    }

    .brand {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;

      gap: 9px;

      font-size: 15px;
      font-weight: 650;

      letter-spacing: -.02em;

      white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .brand img {
      width: 27px;
      height: 27px;

      object-fit: contain;

      filter:
        grayscale(1)
        brightness(0)
        invert(1);

      background: transparent !important;
      border: 0 !important;
      border-radius: 0 !important;
      box-shadow: none !important;
    }

    .header-actions {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;

      gap: 7px;
    }

    .header-link {
      padding: 8px 10px;

      color: #d1d1d5;

      font-size: 13px;
      font-weight: 550;
    }

    .header-enter {
      min-height: 35px;

      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;

      padding: 0 14px;

      border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16);
      border-radius: 8px;

      background: rgba(255,255,255,.055);

      color: #fff;

      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 650;
    }

    /* HERO */

    .hero {
      padding-top: 136px;
      padding-bottom: 70px;
    }

    .eyebrow,
    .section-label {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;

      gap: 8px;

      color: #99999f;

      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;

      letter-spacing: .16em;

      text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      margin-bottom: 22px;
    }

    .eyebrow-dot {
      width: 6px;
      height: 6px;

      border-radius: 50%;

      background: #fff;

      box-shadow: 0 0 16px rgba(255,255,255,.75);
    }

    .hero h1 {
      max-width: 1000px;

      margin: 0;

      font-size: clamp(62px, 8vw, 108px);

      line-height: .91;

      letter-spacing: -.067em;

      font-weight: 680;
    }

    .hero h1 em {
      color: #7d7d82;

      font-style: normal;
      font-weight: 560;
    }

    .hero-lead {
      max-width: 760px;

      margin: 28px 0 0;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: clamp(18px, 1.7vw, 22px);

      line-height: 1.5;

      letter-spacing: -.022em;
    }

    .hero-lead strong {
      color: #f3f3f5;
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    /* CORE STATEMENT */

    .core {
      padding: 54px 0 64px;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .core-grid {
      display: grid;

      grid-template-columns: .65fr 1.35fr;

      gap: 50px;
    }

    .core h2 {
      margin: 0;

      max-width: 800px;

      font-size: clamp(42px, 5vw, 68px);

      line-height: 1;

      letter-spacing: -.052em;

      font-weight: 640;
    }

    .core h2 em {
      color: #7f7f84;

      font-style: normal;
    }

    .core p {
      max-width: 720px;

      margin: 22px 0 0;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 18px;

      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    /* 1:1 */

    .ratio-section {
      padding: 62px 0;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .ratio {
      margin: 18px 0 12px;

      font-size: clamp(100px, 19vw, 230px);

      line-height: .82;

      letter-spacing: -.09em;

      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .ratio-labels {
      max-width: 610px;

      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;

      color: #76767b;

      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;

      letter-spacing: .16em;

      text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .ratio-copy {
      max-width: 730px;

      margin-top: 34px;
    }

    .ratio-copy h2 {
      margin: 0;

      font-size: clamp(38px, 4.6vw, 62px);

      line-height: 1;

      letter-spacing: -.05em;
    }

    .ratio-copy p {
      margin: 20px 0 0;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 18px;

      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    /* FLOW */

    .flow-section {
      padding: 62px 0;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .section-heading {
      max-width: 800px;

      margin: 15px 0 0;

      font-size: clamp(42px, 5vw, 68px);

      line-height: 1;

      letter-spacing: -.052em;

      font-weight: 650;
    }

    .section-intro {
      max-width: 720px;

      margin: 20px 0 0;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 18px;

      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    .flow {
      margin-top: 38px;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .flow-step {
      display: grid;

      grid-template-columns: 70px 250px 1fr;

      gap: 24px;

      padding: 27px 0;

      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .step-number {
      color: #66666b;

      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .flow-step h3 {
      margin: 0;

      font-size: 20px;
      font-weight: 620;

      letter-spacing: -.025em;
    }

    .flow-step p {
      max-width: 650px;

      margin: 0;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 15px;

      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    /* BIOMETRICS */

    .biometrics {
      padding: 66px 0;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .biometric-lead {
      max-width: 790px;

      margin: 21px 0 0;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 19px;

      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    .biometric-grid {
      display: grid;

      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;

      gap: 12px;

      margin-top: 38px;
    }

    .info-card {
      padding: 25px;

      border: 1px solid var(--line);
      border-radius: 16px;

      background:
        linear-gradient(
          145deg,
          rgba(255,255,255,.035),
          rgba(255,255,255,.012)
        );
    }

    .info-card h3 {
      margin: 0;

      font-size: 20px;
      font-weight: 620;

      letter-spacing: -.025em;
    }

    .info-card p {
      margin: 12px 0 0;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 14px;

      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    /* PRIVACY */

    .privacy {
      padding: 66px 0;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .privacy-statement {
      max-width: 970px;

      margin: 17px 0 0;

      font-size: clamp(48px, 6vw, 84px);

      line-height: .96;

      letter-spacing: -.06em;

      font-weight: 650;
    }

    .privacy-statement em {
      color: #77777c;

      font-style: normal;
    }

    .privacy-copy {
      max-width: 740px;

      margin-top: 28px;
    }

    .privacy-copy p {
      margin: 0 0 18px;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 17px;

      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    /* PASSKEY */

    .passkey {
      padding: 66px 0;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .passkey-grid {
      display: grid;

      grid-template-columns: .9fr 1.1fr;

      gap: 58px;
    }

    .passkey h2 {
      margin: 14px 0 0;

      font-size: clamp(42px, 5vw, 68px);

      line-height: 1;

      letter-spacing: -.052em;
    }

    .passkey-copy {
      padding-top: 25px;
    }

    .passkey-copy p {
      margin: 0 0 18px;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 17px;

      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    /* RECOVERY */

    .recovery {
      padding: 66px 0;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .recovery-box {
      margin-top: 32px;

      padding: 30px;

      border: 1px solid var(--line);

      border-radius: 18px;

      background:
        radial-gradient(
          circle at 90% 0%,
          rgba(255,255,255,.06),
          transparent 28%
        ),
        rgba(255,255,255,.02);
    }

    .recovery-box h3 {
      margin: 0;

      font-size: 26px;

      letter-spacing: -.035em;
    }

    .recovery-box p {
      max-width: 720px;

      margin: 14px 0 0;

      color: var(--muted);

      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    /* SECURITY */

    .security {
      padding: 66px 0;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .security-list {
      margin-top: 34px;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .security-row {
      display: grid;

      grid-template-columns: 260px 1fr;

      gap: 35px;

      padding: 23px 0;

      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .security-row strong {
      font-size: 15px;
      font-weight: 620;
    }

    .security-row span {
      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 15px;

      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    /* LIMITS */

    .limits {
      padding: 66px 0;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .limits h2 {
      max-width: 950px;

      margin: 16px 0 0;

      font-size: clamp(45px, 5.8vw, 78px);

      line-height: .97;

      letter-spacing: -.058em;

      font-weight: 650;
    }

    .limits h2 em {
      color: #77777c;
      font-style: normal;
    }

    .limits-copy {
      max-width: 750px;

      margin-top: 26px;
    }

    .limits-copy p {
      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 17px;

      line-height: 1.62;
    }

    /* FINAL */

    .final {
      padding: 72px 0 62px;

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);

      text-align: center;
    }

    .final h2 {
      max-width: 850px;

      margin: 15px auto 0;

      font-size: clamp(52px, 7vw, 94px);

      line-height: .91;

      letter-spacing: -.067em;
    }

    .final h2 em {
      color: #7c7c81;
      font-style: normal;
    }

    .final p {
      max-width: 650px;

      margin: 23px auto 0;

      color: var(--muted);

      font-size: 17px;

      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    .final-actions {
      margin-top: 27px;

      display: flex;
      justify-content: center;

      gap: 9px;

      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    .button {
      min-height: 46px;

      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;

      padding: 0 18px;

      border-radius: 9px;

      font-size: 13px;
      font-weight: 650;
    }

    .button-primary {
      background: #fff;
      color: #000;
    }

    .button-secondary {
      border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.15);

      background: rgba(255,255,255,.035);

      color: #fff;
    }

    /* FOOTER */

    .site-footer {
      padding:
        22px 0
        calc(22px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom));

      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);

      color: #77777c;

      font-size: 12px;
    }

    .footer-inner {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
      align-items: center;

      gap: 15px;

      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    /* MOBILE */

    @media (max-width: 820px) {
      .wrap {
        width: calc(100% - 24px);
      }

      .header-inner {
        height: 56px;
      }

      .brand {
        font-size: 13px;
      }

      .brand img {
        width: 25px;
        height: 25px;
      }

      .header-link {
        display: none;
      }

      .header-enter {
        min-height: 32px;

        padding: 0 11px;

        font-size: 11px;
      }

      .hero {
        padding-top: 94px;
        padding-bottom: 46px;
      }

      .eyebrow {
        margin-bottom: 16px;

        font-size: 9px;
      }

      .hero h1 {
        max-width: 590px;

        font-size: clamp(51px, 14vw, 76px);
      }

      .hero-lead {
        margin-top: 20px;

        font-size: 16px;
      }

      .core,
      .ratio-section,
      .flow-section,
      .biometrics,
      .privacy,
      .passkey,
      .recovery,
      .security,
      .limits {
        padding: 48px 0;
      }

      .core-grid,
      .passkey-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;

        gap: 20px;
      }

      .core h2,
      .section-heading,
      .passkey h2 {
        font-size: clamp(39px, 10.5vw, 55px);
      }

      .core p,
      .section-intro,
      .biometric-lead {
        margin-top: 17px;

        font-size: 15px;
      }

      .ratio {
        margin-top: 18px;

        font-size: clamp(100px, 34vw, 155px);
      }

      .ratio-labels {
        max-width: 410px;
      }

      .ratio-copy {
        margin-top: 28px;
      }

      .ratio-copy h2 {
        font-size: clamp(36px, 9.5vw, 49px);
      }

      .ratio-copy p {
        font-size: 15px;
      }

      .flow {
        margin-top: 28px;
      }

      .flow-step {
        grid-template-columns: 38px 1fr;

        gap: 12px;

        padding: 22px 0;
      }

      .flow-step p {
        grid-column: 2;

        font-size: 14px;
      }

      .flow-step h3 {
        font-size: 18px;
      }

      .biometric-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;

        margin-top: 28px;
      }

      .info-card {
        padding: 20px;

        border-radius: 14px;
      }

      .privacy-statement {
        font-size: clamp(43px, 11.5vw, 60px);
      }

      .privacy-copy {
        margin-top: 22px;
      }

      .privacy-copy p,
      .passkey-copy p,
      .limits-copy p {
        font-size: 15px;
      }

      .passkey-copy {
        padding-top: 0;
      }

      .recovery-box {
        margin-top: 25px;

        padding: 21px;

        border-radius: 14px;
      }

      .security-list {
        margin-top: 27px;
      }

      .security-row {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;

        gap: 8px;

        padding: 20px 0;
      }

      .security-row span {
        font-size: 14px;
      }

      .limits h2 {
        font-size: clamp(41px, 10.5vw, 57px);
      }

      .final {
        padding: 52px 0 48px;
      }

      .final h2 {
        font-size: clamp(49px, 13vw, 70px);
      }

      .final p {
        font-size: 15px;
      }

      .footer-inner {
        justify-content: center;

        text-align: center;
      }
    }

    @media (max-width: 430px) {
      .wrap {
        width: calc(100% - 18px);
      }

      .hero {
        padding-top: 86px;
        padding-bottom: 40px;
      }

      .hero h1 {
        font-size: clamp(47px, 13.5vw, 60px);
      }

      .core,
      .ratio-section,
      .flow-section,
      .biometrics,
      .privacy,
      .passkey,
      .recovery,
      .security,
      .limits {
        padding: 43px 0;
      }

      .button {
        width: 100%;

        max-width: 330px;
      }
    }
  </style>
</head>

<body>

  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="wrap header-inner">

      <a class="brand" href="index.html">
        <img src="logo.png" alt="Hash Humanity logo" />
        <span>Hash Humanity</span>
      </a>

      <nav class="header-actions">
        <a class="header-link" href="mission.html">
          Mission
        </a>

        <a
          class="header-enter"
          href="https://hashhumanity.world/universe"
        >
          Enter
        </a>
      </nav>

    </div>
  </header>


  <main>

    <!-- HERO -->

    <section class="hero wrap">

      <div class="eyebrow">
        <span class="eyebrow-dot"></span>
        HUMANKEY PROTOCOL
      </div>

      <h1>
        Prove you are human.<br />
        <em>Not who you are.</em>
      </h1>

      <p class="hero-lead">
        HumanKey is the proof-of-personhood system behind Hash Humanity.
        It combines liveness detection, biometric uniqueness checks,
        privacy-preserving account controls, and passkeys to support one
        simple rule: <strong>one human, one account.</strong>
      </p>

    </section>


    <!-- WHAT IT IS -->

    <section class="core">

      <div class="wrap core-grid">

        <div>
          <span class="section-label">
            WHAT HUMANKEY DOES
          </span>
        </div>

        <div>

          <h2>
            We need to know that you are a person.
            <em>We do not need your public identity.</em>
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most websites prove that someone controls an email address,
            phone number, password, or device. That does not prove that
            every account belongs to a different human being. HumanKey
            approaches the problem differently. Before an account is
            established, the system checks for a live human and checks
            whether that person appears to have already enrolled.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ONE HUMAN -->

    <section class="ratio-section">

      <div class="wrap">

        <span class="section-label">
          THE RULE
        </span>

        <div class="ratio">
          1:1
        </div>

        <div class="ratio-labels">
          <span>ONE HUMAN</span>
          <span>ONE ACCOUNT</span>
        </div>

        <div class="ratio-copy">

          <h2>
            Another email should not mean another identity.
          </h2>

          <p>
            Creating another email address is easy. Creating another
            username is easy. Software can automate both. HumanKey is
            designed so that creating another account requires another
            unique human being, not simply another credential.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- FLOW -->

    <section class="flow-section">

      <div class="wrap">

        <span class="section-label">
          HOW IT WORKS
        </span>

        <h2 class="section-heading">
          From person to HumanKey.
        </h2>

        <p class="section-intro">
          HumanKey separates proof of personhood from everyday
          authentication. The biometric process establishes human
          uniqueness during enrollment. After enrollment, the account
          can use a passkey for normal authentication.
        </p>


        <div class="flow">

          <div class="flow-step">
            <span class="step-number">01</span>

            <h3>Consent</h3>

            <p>
              Biometric processing begins only after the user is shown
              the HumanKey process and provides consent. The camera is
              not intended to perform silent background verification.
            </p>
          </div>


          <div class="flow-step">
            <span class="step-number">02</span>

            <h3>Liveness</h3>

            <p>
              The user completes a liveness session. The purpose is to
              determine whether the camera is seeing a live participant
              rather than simply accepting a photograph or basic replay
              as proof of a person.
            </p>
          </div>


          <div class="flow-step">
            <span class="step-number">03</span>

            <h3>Face representation</h3>

            <p>
              A facial image that passes the required checks can be used
              to create or obtain a mathematical representation suitable
              for biometric comparison. This representation allows the
              system to compare faces without treating a username or
              email address as proof of uniqueness.
            </p>
          </div>


          <div class="flow-step">
            <span class="step-number">04</span>

            <h3>Uniqueness search</h3>

            <p>
              The new enrollment is checked against previously enrolled
              biometric records. The purpose is not to discover the
              person's legal identity. The question is whether this
              appears to be the same person who already enrolled.
            </p>
          </div>


          <div class="flow-step">
            <span class="step-number">05</span>

            <h3>HumanKey established</h3>

            <p>
              When the required checks pass, the account can be marked
              as having satisfied HumanKey's proof-of-personhood
              requirements. The social account does not need to publicly
              expose the person's legal name simply because personhood
              was verified.
            </p>
          </div>


          <div class="flow-step">
            <span class="step-number">06</span>

            <h3>Passkey created</h3>

            <p>
              The user creates a passkey for everyday authentication.
              Passkeys use public-key cryptography. HumanKey therefore
              does not need to perform a new facial uniqueness scan every
              time the person signs in.
            </p>
          </div>


          <div class="flow-step">
            <span class="step-number">07</span>

            <h3>Enter Hash Humanity</h3>

            <p>
              The account enters the network with a HumanKey-backed
              personhood state. The goal is not to certify what the user
              believes or says. It is to make it much harder for one
              operator to appear to be an unlimited number of independent
              people.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- BIOMETRICS -->

    <section class="biometrics">

      <div class="wrap">

        <span class="section-label">
          BIOMETRICS
        </span>

        <h2 class="section-heading">
          Why HumanKey uses a face.
        </h2>

        <p class="biometric-lead">
          A biometric characteristic is useful here because it is tied
          to a physical person in a way that an email address, username,
          password, or IP address is not. HumanKey uses facial biometrics
          for a narrow purpose: establishing liveness and helping determine
          whether the same person has already enrolled.
        </p>


        <div class="biometric-grid">

          <article class="info-card">
            <h3>Liveness is not face recognition</h3>

            <p>
              These are related but different jobs. Liveness asks whether
              a real person appears to be physically present during the
              verification session. Face comparison asks whether two
              biometric representations are sufficiently similar.
              HumanKey needs both concepts because proving that someone is
              live does not, by itself, prove that the person has not
              already created another account.
            </p>
          </article>


          <article class="info-card">
            <h3>Biometric matching is probabilistic</h3>

            <p>
              Facial comparison does not produce mathematical certainty.
              Biometric systems work with similarity and confidence
              measurements. Thresholds determine when a result is strong
              enough to accept, reject, or require another attempt.
              HumanKey should therefore be understood as a security system
              designed to make duplicate enrollment substantially harder,
              not as an infallible measurement.
            </p>
          </article>


          <article class="info-card">
            <h3>The face is not the profile</h3>

            <p>
              Passing HumanKey does not require the social profile to
              display the person's face. A user can choose how they present
              themselves publicly. The biometric process exists to support
              personhood and uniqueness checks, not to force a public
              identity onto the account.
            </p>
          </article>


          <article class="info-card">
            <h3>The purpose is uniqueness</h3>

            <p>
              HumanKey is not designed to look at a face and determine a
              person's name. Its purpose is narrower. It needs to answer
              whether a live person is present and whether the biometric
              evidence indicates that the person has already enrolled.
            </p>
          </article>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DATA -->

    <section class="privacy">

      <div class="wrap">

        <span class="section-label">
          BIOMETRIC DATA
        </span>

        <h2 class="privacy-statement">
          The system needs enough information to recognize
          <em>the same human again.</em>
        </h2>


        <div class="privacy-copy">

          <p>
            Duplicate-person detection requires a biometric reference
            that can be compared against a later enrollment. That is
            different from keeping a public photograph or using a face
            as ordinary profile information.
          </p>

          <p>
            The biometric reference exists for a specific security
            purpose: uniqueness. Access to biometric information should
            be limited to the systems that perform enrollment, duplicate
            detection, and authorized recovery functions.
          </p>

          <p>
            HumanKey does not need a person's face every time they read a
            post, write a comment, send a message, or sign in. Once the
            HumanKey process has established the account and the user has
            created a passkey, normal authentication can use that passkey.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- PASSKEY -->

    <section class="passkey">

      <div class="wrap passkey-grid">

        <div>

          <span class="section-label">
            PASSKEYS
          </span>

          <h2>
            Your face establishes personhood.
            Your passkey gets you back in.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="passkey-copy">

          <p>
            HumanKey separates enrollment from everyday authentication.
            Biometrics are valuable for establishing that a live and
            apparently unique person is enrolling. They do not need to
            become the password for every session.
          </p>

          <p>
            Passkeys use public-key credentials. The service receives
            cryptographic proof from the authenticator rather than relying
            on a reusable password being sent to the server.
          </p>

          <p>
            This separation matters. The biometric system answers the
            personhood question. The passkey handles routine account
            authentication.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- RECOVERY -->

    <section class="recovery">

      <div class="wrap">

        <span class="section-label">
          ACCOUNT RECOVERY
        </span>

        <h2 class="section-heading">
          Losing a device should not mean losing yourself.
        </h2>

        <p class="section-intro">
          Recovery is one of the reasons HumanKey keeps personhood
          separate from a particular phone or computer. If an authorized
          recovery process requires biometric verification, HumanKey can
          use the enrolled person's biometric relationship as part of
          establishing that the person requesting recovery is the same
          human who originally enrolled.
        </p>


        <div class="recovery-box">

          <h3>Recovery does not create another human.</h3>

          <p>
            A successful recovery should restore access to the existing
            human account rather than create a second identity. After the
            required recovery checks succeed, a new authentication
            credential can be established for the same account.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SECURITY -->

    <section class="security">

      <div class="wrap">

        <span class="section-label">
          WHAT HUMANKEY IS DEFENDING AGAINST
        </span>

        <h2 class="section-heading">
          The problem is not just bots. It is cheap identity.
        </h2>


        <div class="security-list">

          <div class="security-row">
            <strong>Automated account creation</strong>

            <span>
              Software can generate usernames, email addresses, and
              registration attempts at machine speed. Requiring a live,
              unique person introduces a physical constraint that ordinary
              account creation does not have.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="security-row">
            <strong>Duplicate enrollment</strong>

            <span>
              A person attempting to create another HumanKey-backed
              account can be checked against previously enrolled biometric
              records instead of being treated as new simply because a
              different email address was supplied.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="security-row">
            <strong>Photo and replay attempts</strong>

            <span>
              Liveness detection exists to distinguish a live participant
              from presentation attacks such as simply placing an image
              in front of a camera. No liveness system should be described
              as impossible to defeat, so HumanKey treats it as one layer
              in the enrollment decision.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="security-row">
            <strong>Disposable identities</strong>

            <span>
              If creating another identity requires another unique human,
              repeatedly replacing banned or discarded accounts becomes
              more expensive than simply generating another credential.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="security-row">
            <strong>Credential theft</strong>

            <span>
              Passkeys reduce dependence on reusable passwords. WebAuthn
              credentials are scoped to the relying party and use
              public-key authentication rather than sending the same
              password to a server on every login.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="security-row">
            <strong>Large-scale manufactured consensus</strong>

            <span>
              HumanKey cannot stop real people from coordinating. What it
              is designed to constrain is the ability of one person or one
              automated system to cheaply present itself as a large crowd
              of independent humans.
            </span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- LIMITS -->

    <section class="limits">

      <div class="wrap">

        <span class="section-label">
          WHAT HUMANKEY DOES NOT DO
        </span>

        <h2>
          Being human does not automatically make someone
          <em>right, honest, or decent.</em>
        </h2>


        <div class="limits-copy">

          <p>
            HumanKey is proof of personhood, not proof of character.
            A verified human can lie. A verified human can spread bad
            information. Real people can harass each other, coordinate
            campaigns, make mistakes, or behave badly.
          </p>

          <p>
            HumanKey does not decide what someone is allowed to believe.
            It does not determine whether an opinion is true. It does not
            turn disagreement into consensus.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its job is much more specific. It is designed to make it
            harder for one operator to manufacture an unlimited number of
            apparently independent people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hash Humanity is not trying to build a network where everyone
            agrees. It is trying to build one where the people disagreeing
            are actually people.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


